# ============================================
# Kaleido Finance - Environment Variables
# ============================================
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your values
# cp .env.example .env

# ============================================
# Contract Addresses (single-chain write path — being retired)
# ============================================
# These five are the app's OLD address source and every one of them shipped here
# with an Abstract Testnet (11124) literal, from a deployment that no longer
# exists. They are blank now, deliberately: an address that is present but dead
# is worse than one that is absent, because nothing checks it. validateAddress
# (src/config/contracts.ts) runs inside getKaleidoContract / getKLDVaultContract
# / getKaleidoMasterChefContract rather than at import, so a blank produces
# "Missing contract address for <name>" at the write path — a legible error, at
# the moment it matters. A stale literal instead produces a transaction sent to a
# codeless address on whatever chain the wallet happens to be on.
#
# The replacement is DEPLOYMENTS in src/constants/registry.ts, keyed by chain id
# and generated by `npm run gen:registry` from the deploy scripts' output. Fill
# these in only for a single-chain bring-up; the multichain path does not read
# them. They disappear as their importers move to getContracts(chainId).

# Main Diamond contract - EIP-2535 Diamond Standard
NEXT_PUBLIC_KALEIDO_DIAMOND_ADDRESS=

# Deployer/signer private key used by scripts and the off-chain server.
# NEVER commit a real key. Provide it via your local, untracked .env file.
PRIVATE_KEY=your_private_key_here

# Token Faucet contract for claiming test tokens. NOT an address source any more:
# /faucet, useFaucet and Luca's claimTestTokens tool all read
# DEPLOYMENTS[chainId].faucet, written by `npm run gen:registry` from
# smart-contract/deployment-faucet-<network>.json. Deploy it with
# `npm run deploy:faucet` from smart-contract/.
#
# Still read by one thing: Luca's address auditor, which refuses a plain `send`
# into any contract this deployment owns. Setting it there only widens that
# refusal set, so a stale value here cannot misdirect a transaction.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TOKENFAUCET_ADDRESS=

# NEXT_PUBLIC_KLD_VAULT_ADDRESS stood here and is gone. It was never set, which
# meant /stake was unreachable on every chain, and there was nothing to set it to
# because no KLD ERC20 existed. The vault is deployed per chain now, so staking
# reads DEPLOYMENTS[chainId].kldVault alongside .kld and .stKLD, written by
# `npm run gen:registry` from smart-contract/deployment-kld-<network>.json.
# Deploy the set with `npx hardhat run scripts/deploy-kld.js` from
# smart-contract/. One env var cannot describe five chains, which is the whole
# reason it went.

# Protocol contract address. Read only by getProtocolContract and Luca's address
# auditor; on a Diamond deploy this is the same address as the Diamond above,
# since ProtocolFacet is reached through it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PROTOCOL_ADDRESS=

# MasterChef, for farm emissions. Not deployed this wave. The contract itself is
# clean — it takes `IERC20 _kld` in its constructor — but scripts/
# deploy-masterchef.js:11 still hardcodes the retired Abstract KLD address, so
# deploying it means threading the registry through that script first.
# Documented here because envVars.ts reads it and used to fall back to a
# hardcoded Abstract literal when unset — which is why a blank was not
# previously enough to disable it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MASTER_CHEF_ADDRESS=

# OUR PythPriceOracle wrapper, not Pyth's own contract — the address
# scripts/deploy-oracle.js prints. The distinction matters and is not cosmetic:
# both have code and both answer getPrice(bytes32), so pointing this at Pyth's
# contract reads prices against Pyth's own validity window instead of the
# staleness and confidence bounds this protocol configures. See PYTH_CONTRACT vs
# PYTH_PRICE_ORACLE in smart-contract/.env.example.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PYTH_ORACLE_ADDRESS=

# ============================================
# Read chain and RPC URLs
# ============================================
# Which chain the wallet-less read path describes. /pool's protocol-wide stats,
# /api/market/overview and Luca's read tools all render one chain's view, and
# there is no wallet to ask which — so it is configuration, and this is it.
#
# Unset takes the default (11155111, Sepolia, step 1 of TESTNET_WAVE). Set it to
# any chain id registered in src/constants/chains.ts. A value that is set but
# unparseable or unregistered throws at startup on purpose: that is an operator
# typo worth one edit, whereas falling back silently would serve one chain's
# figures to an operator who believed they were seeing another's.
#
# It used to be a hardcoded 11124 (Abstract Testnet) in src/config/provider.ts.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_, so it is inlined at build time — changing it needs a dev-server
# restart.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_READ_CHAIN_ID=11155111

# Optional override for the read chain's HTTP endpoint. Leave it unset and the
# URL is derived from NEXT_PUBLIC_READ_CHAIN_ID via chains.ts, which is the safer
# default: the provider is constructed with `staticNetwork: true`, so ethers never
# calls eth_chainId and cannot notice a URL pointing at a different chain than the
# id it was handed. Every read would return real data about the wrong chain, and
# registry lookups keyed by the declared id would pair it with another chain's
# addresses.
#
# Set this only for a private or rate-limited node, and then it is your job to
# point it at a node serving NEXT_PUBLIC_READ_CHAIN_ID.
NEXT_PUBLIC_HTTP_RPC=

# WebSocket endpoint for chain event subscriptions, on the same chain as above.
# Unlike the HTTP URL this one cannot be derived — chains.ts lists HTTP endpoints
# only, and a WebSocket endpoint is a different URL, not the same one over wss://.
# Unset means the app simply does not subscribe; polling still works.
NEXT_PUBLIC_WEBSOCKET_RPC=

# Read by the referral route only (src/app/api/referral/route.ts), which falls
# back to NEXT_PUBLIC_HTTP_RPC when it is unset. Set it only if referral signing
# has to use a different endpoint from the read path.
NEXT_PUBLIC_HTTP_RPC_AB=

# ============================================
# Third-party Services
# ============================================
# Thirdweb Client ID (for wallet connection)
NEXT_PUBLIC_THIRDWEB_CLIENT_KEY=your_thirdweb_client_id_here

# Supabase URL (for database)
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co

# Supabase Anon Key (public API key)
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_KEY=your_supabase_anon_key_here

# Supabase service-role key. SERVER ONLY — it bypasses row-level security, so
# anyone holding it can read and write every table. Never prefix it with
# NEXT_PUBLIC_. Without it, Luca's model-request quota is not enforced and the
# agent runs unmetered.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Model requests each wallet may spend per UTC day. Direct commands are free and
# never counted, so this only rations provider calls. Defaults to 25.
# AGENT_DAILY_MODEL_REQUESTS=25

# The notional ceiling a client cannot raise, in USD. Defaults to 25000.
#
# `limits` arrives in the request body, so it is user input in the literal sense:
# a caller can post maxPerAction: 1e12 and the user's own setting stops meaning
# anything. User limits may only ever TIGHTEN, and this is the number they tighten
# from — see HARD_MAX_NOTIONAL_USD in src/lib/ai/auditor.ts. Server-only, and it
# is not the security boundary: the user signs every transaction and
# AgentPermissionFacet enforces its own bounds on-chain.
# AGENT_MAX_NOTIONAL_USD=25000

# ============================================
# Twitter/X Authentication
# ============================================
# Twitter OAuth Client ID
NEXT_PUBLIC_TWITTER_CLIENT_ID=your_twitter_client_id_here

# Twitter OAuth Redirect URI
NEXT_PUBLIC_TWITTER_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback

# Twitter API Key
# The OAuth2 client secret, used for the HTTP Basic header on X's token
# exchange. NOT public: this was NEXT_PUBLIC_TWITTER_KEY, which Next inlined
# into the browser bundle. If a deployment still has that variable set, remove
# it — the value it held is compromised and needs regenerating in the X
# developer portal, because it has been served to every visitor.
TWITTER_CLIENT_SECRET=your_twitter_oauth_client_secret_here

# ============================================
# Analytics
# ============================================
# Google Analytics Measurement ID (optional)
NEXT_PUBLIC_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-XXXXXXXXXX

# ============================================
# AI providers (Luca)
# ============================================
# Luca picks a provider from whichever key is set. Set AI_PROVIDER to force
# one, or leave it unset and the first configured key wins (AgentRouter, then
# Claude, then OpenAI). Adding a provider is one adapter in src/lib/ai/providers.
#
# Server-side only — never prefix these with NEXT_PUBLIC_, or the key ships
# to every visitor in the browser bundle.
#
# Pin this rather than relying on precedence if more than one key is ever set:
# unset means AgentRouter wins, and see the warning under it for why that is
# not what you want.
# AI_PROVIDER=claude

# AgentRouter — external model router. A reseller: one key, and the model id
# decides whether the request lands at Anthropic or OpenAI on the far side. It
# exposes BOTH wire formats, through two adapters, so AI_PROVIDER picks one:
#   AI_PROVIDER=agentrouter         -> /v1/messages         (ClaudeProvider)
#   AI_PROVIDER=agentrouter-openai  -> /v1/chat/completions (OpenAIProvider)
# A key alone cannot express which you meant, which is why the fall-through
# picks the Messages path. BASE_URL is origin-only for the first ("/v1/messages"
# is appended); the OpenAI one carries its own "/v1".
#
# Model ids are per-token — call GET /v1/models with your own key rather than
# trusting a docs page. An id your token lacks returns 403
# "该令牌无权访问模型 <id>", not 404, so a typo and an entitlement gap look alike.
#
# Selectable ids live in ROUTER_MODELS (src/lib/ai/index.ts), which maps each id
# to the adapter its wire format needs. A request may name one and it overrides
# AI_PROVIDER; anything not in that table is dropped rather than forwarded, so
# an id your key lacks cannot spend a metered request to fail. Update that table
# when your entitlements change — the vars here only pick the default.
#
# AGENTROUTER_USER_AGENT is required and is the part to think about before
# enabling this. The router allowlists client User-Agents: with identical bodies
# it returns 401 "unauthorized client detected" for an app UA and for none, and
# 200 only for Claude Code's. Setting it means every request from this app
# claims to be a client it is not, to pass a check the provider added on
# purpose. A service reselling frontier models this cheaply while gated to one
# CLI is plausibly proxying subscriptions against the upstream vendor's terms —
# so the risk is an account cut off without notice, and a tightened check
# breaking Luca in production with no change on our side. Capability was never
# the issue: tool calls come back correctly formed on both paths. A direct
# vendor key below is the recommendation.
# AGENTROUTER_API_KEY=
# AGENTROUTER_USER_AGENT=
# AGENTROUTER_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# AGENTROUTER_BASE_URL=https://agentrouter.org
# AGENTROUTER_OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5.6-sol
# AGENTROUTER_OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://agentrouter.org/v1

# Direct vendor key — the recommended path. console.anthropic.com
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5

# Any Chat-Completions-compatible gateway — OpenRouter, LiteLLM, Azure, a local
# proxy — is these three variables and no code change. Include the version
# segment; "/chat/completions" is appended. Unset means api.openai.com/v1.
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
#
# Optional cap, sent as max_completion_tokens (gpt-5 and o-series reject the
# older max_tokens). Unset means the model's own maximum, which is the default.
# OPENAI_MAX_TOKENS=8192
#
# Overrides the User-Agent, for gateways that allowlist client UAs. See the
# AgentRouter note above for why setting this is a decision, not a default.
# OPENAI_USER_AGENT=

# Legacy external AI engine. Used only when no provider key above is set.
# AI_ENGINE_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000
# AI_ENGINE_TIMEOUT=300000

# ============================================
# Web push (notification centre)
# ============================================
# Generate the pair once with:  npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
#
# The public key is *designed* to be public — it is the applicationServerKey the
# browser sends to the push service to prove later pushes came from us. The
# private key signs them, so it is server-only and must never gain a
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
#
# With neither set, push simply does not offer itself: pushSupported() returns
# false, the panel hides the enable button, and in-app plus page-level OS
# notifications still work. Nothing breaks; the browser-closed rung is missing.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Contact address a push service can reach if our sends misbehave. Some services
# reject a subscription without one.
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:support@kaleidofinance.xyz

# Shared secret guarding POST /api/push/send.
#
# That route writes to users' lock screens with the app's own icon attached, so
# leaving it open would be a phishing primitive, not merely a spam relay. The
# route refuses to send at all while this is unset rather than falling open.
# Generate with:  openssl rand -hex 32
# PUSH_SEND_SECRET=

# --- Push watcher (scripts/push-watcher.mjs, .github/workflows/push-watcher.yml) ---
# The server-side job that fires a push when the browser is closed: it scans the
# loan events on every deployed chain and POSTs /api/push/send for the affected
# wallet. It is NOT part of the Next app — it runs from CI (or by hand) — so these
# are plain env vars, not NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#
# APP_URL is the origin of the deployed app the watcher POSTs to. With APP_URL or
# PUSH_SEND_SECRET unset the watcher runs as a dry run: it logs the pushes it would
# send and sends none. PUSH_SEND_SECRET must equal the value above that the app was
# given, or the route returns 401. The checkpoint store and the subscribed-wallet
# prefilter reuse SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY + NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL; without them
# the watcher re-scans a recent window and cannot broadcast safely.
# APP_URL=https://app.kaleidofinance.xyz

# Origin of the external notification engine, which serves the inbound half of
# the inbox: GET /notifications/history and a WebSocket at /ws/receiver. Unset
# means no transport and no attempt at one — locally-raised notifications still
# work, and the browser does not pay a WebSocket that reconnects forever against
# a host serving neither route. Nothing has ever set this; the seam stays for when
# the engine is wired.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE=

# ============================================
# Notes
# ============================================
# - All NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables are exposed to the browser
# - Never put sensitive keys in NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables
# - For production, use environment-specific values
# - Contract addresses do NOT belong here any more. They live in DEPLOYMENTS
#   (src/constants/registry.ts), keyed by chain id and written by
#   `npm run gen:registry` from the deploy scripts' JSON output. The five
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_*_ADDRESS variables at the top are the single-chain path being
#   retired; this file used to state they were "for Abstract Testnet (Chain ID:
#   11124)" and to say "for other networks, update addresses accordingly", which
#   is exactly the per-chain hand-editing the registry exists to prevent.

# --- MoonPay: fiat on-ramp (Buy) and off-ramp (Sell) ---
# Publishable key. Safe in the browser; it identifies your MoonPay account.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MOONPAY_API_KEY=
# Secret key. SERVER ONLY — it signs widget URLs, so anyone holding it can mint
# signed URLs against your account. Never prefix this with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
MOONPAY_SECRET_KEY=
# "live" switches to buy.moonpay.com / sell.moonpay.com. Anything else stays
# on the sandbox, which is where you should verify the signature first.
MOONPAY_ENV=sandbox

# --- CoinGecko: price history for the trade chart ---
# Optional. /api/prices works without it on the public rate limit (~10-30 calls
# per minute, shared by everyone on the host's IP) and serves its last cached
# response when that limit is hit, so the chart degrades to slightly-behind
# rather than blank. A demo key raises the ceiling.
#
# SERVER ONLY, deliberately. There is no NEXT_PUBLIC_ form and there should not
# be: the key identifies the account being billed, and calling the feed from the
# browser would also spend each user's own IP budget instead of one shared cache.
# COINGECKO_API_KEY=

# ============================================
# Demo fixtures (pre-deployment interface audit)
# ============================================
# Serves invented rows in place of contract reads, so every product surface can
# be checked before the first deploy. NOTHING IS DEPLOYED ON ANY CHAIN TODAY —
# DEPLOYMENTS (src/constants/registry.ts) is empty on purpose, so getContracts()
# returns {} everywhere and every protocol read comes back null. That is honest
# and it is also unverifiable: an empty table looks identical whether the page is
# correct, half-written, or throwing.
#
# Set to 1 or true and /pool, /pool/positions, /stake, /stable, /borrow, /lend,
# /myloans, /mylends and /portfolio all fill in, and ChainGate's empty-state panel
# steps aside. Any other value — including unset, the production case — leaves it
# fully off.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO, which is the point: only reads are substituted. Every
# write still resolves the real contract, so pressing Stake or Mint in demo mode
# produces the real failure, which is the answer the audit wants. /leaderboard is
# database-backed and untouched by this flag; swap quotes and the order book have
# no fixtures either.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_, so it is inlined into the browser bundle at build time — it is a
# display switch, not a secret. Changing it needs a dev-server restart, and a
# production bundle must never be built with it set. Nothing in the UI marks these
# numbers as invented, by design: the app has to read as the finished product, so
# this line is the only thing standing between fixtures and production. See
# src/lib/mock/index.ts for the three-step removal procedure.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MOCK_DATA=1

